return [====[osition:100% 0%"></div><p>Staging comes before Dungeon 1 and after each successful rescue. Speak to the Hermit, inspect your character in the mirror, check the Heroes of Legend board, and visit the Debbie statue for your wallet collection.</p><p>Your first Hermit visit issues one identity-bound advanced starter weapon; reopening the conversation does not issue another. Later staging visits offer one standard random-drop gift. Finish pending class and feat decisions, then choose your loadout.</p><p>Rescuing Deborah resets the collapse clock to 30:00. It stays paused through the celebration and your return to staging, until the first Hero enters the next dungeon. Your weapons, magazines, reserve ammunition, equipped gear, stored items, and consumable stacks stay with your Hero.</p><p>The portal commits you to the active dungeon. A Hero who remains behind cannot pause the countdown for teammates already inside.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 6</div></article><article class="page" id="clock" data-title="Thirty minutes. One campaign."><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Thirty minutes. One campaign.</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>The campaign begins with 1,800 seconds. Its clock starts exactly once, when the first Hero commits to entering the labyrinth. A successful rescue advances the dungeon; it does not start a new timer.</p><p>Death, Tetris, menus, map use, staging visits, spectating, and disconnections do not pause or extend this clock. An empty server does not bank extra time for your return.</p><p>At zero, TIME OVER ends the campaign and the prison collapses. A fresh campaign starts automatically 20 seconds after the collapse finishes. After five seconds of aftermath, you can press E to restart sooner at Dungeon 1 with a fresh clock and roster.</p><p>A rescue completed earlier in the campaign does not exempt the party from the eventual timeout.</p><blockquote>“I appreciate the sightseeing. I would appreciate the rescue more.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 7</div></article><article class="page" id="route" data-title="Follow the keycards"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Follow the keycards</h2><table><tr><td>1 · Red</td><td>Find Red Keycard → open Red Gate</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Blue</td><td>Find Blue Keycard → open Blue Gate</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Yellow</td><td>Find Yellow Keycard → open Yellow Gate</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Black</td><td>Find Neil → defeat Neil → take Black Keycard</td></tr><tr><td>5 · The Warden</td><td>Open Black Gate → enter Gordon&#x27;s arena</td></tr><tr><td>6 · Deborah</td><td>Defeat Gordon → take Jail Key → open jail → touch Deborah</td></tr></table><p>The labyrinth spans connected floors, stairs, passages, and gates. Its required route is solvable; a gate is a progression barrier, not a wall to bypass with a movement trick.</p><p>Watch the current objective and your floor. A target above or below you may require the stairs, not a turn into the nearest corridor. Defeating Brute alone never awards the Black Keycard.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 8</div></article><article class="page" id="map" data-title="Read the maze"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the maze</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 0%"></div><p>Press M for your map. It consumes Magic while open and suppresses Magic regeneration. At the base rate, a full 100 Magic lasts 15 seconds; Wisdom and applicable equipment or feats alter the cost. At zero Magic the map closes.</p><p>Use short looks to check your floor, route, and current objective. The map is information, not a pause screen. Your build determines available breadcrumbs and other navigation aids.</p><p>GPS requires its feat. Press G to toggle its private voice without spending Magic. When enabled, stand still for one second with no living enemies within two cells on your floor. It gives a direction, then can repeat after four seconds of silence. Movement, combat, or nearby enemies cancel the cue—even if those enemies are behind walls.</p><p>GPS follows the current legal objective route. It does not reveal loot, monsters, or future objectives. A server with visual maps disabled can still allow GPS.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 9</div></article><article class="page" id="character" data-title="Who are you this time?"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Who are you this time?</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>Your Hero has a generated name, appearance, Origin, Background, and Motive. Those three traits each grant a permanent perk: a favored weapon, a favored enemy, or ability points. Each chooses independently; matching perks can stack.</p><p>An ability perk gives either +2 to one ability or +1 to each of two different abilities. Read the actual trait on your Character Sheet to see what your Hero received.</p><p>Your starting scores use six rolls of four six-sided dice, dropping each lowest die. The initial array must total at least 74. A stored weakness pass then reduces the weakest score by 2d2 and the next two by 1d3 each. There is no second reroll after these reductions.</p><p>Death, reconnection, and dungeon transitions preserve your identity, class, growth, XP, and feats. A true campaign restart creates a fresh roster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 10</div></article><article class="page" id="abilities" data-title="Six scores, many surprises"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Six scores, many surprises</h2><table><tr><td>STR · Strength</td><td>Physical damage; Push; Fighter shield bonus</td></tr><tr><td>DEX · Dexterity</td><td>Movement and applicable attack handling; Clumsy and fire saves</td></tr><tr><td>CON · Constitution</td><td>HP growth, damage resistance, regeneration; poison and bleeding saves</td></tr><tr><td>INT · Intelligence</td><td>Magic regeneration and Arcane Shield integrity</td></tr><tr><td>WIS · Wisdom</td><td>Magic damage, approved range and utility scaling; Magic saves</td></tr><tr><td>CHA · Charisma</td><td>Morale and personality effects</td></tr></table><p>Scores stay within 3–30. An ability modifier is floor((score − 10) / 2): 10–11 gives +0, 12–13 gives +1, and 20–21 gives +5.</p><p>Equipment changes your active combat scores, but temporary gear does not qualify you for feats. Feat requirements use your permanent character abilities. The sheet distinguishes your build from what your equipped items contribute.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 11</div></article><article class="page" id="fighter" data-title="Fighter: stay in the fight"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Fighter: stay in the fight</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><p>A Fighter regenerates toward 33% of maximum HP from Level 1. After five continuous seconds without effective damage, regeneration starts at 1% of maximum HP per second, modified by Constitution. Damage restarts the delay. This does not refill temporary Tetris overfill.</p><p>Second Wind, Rapid Recovery, and Unbroken add 11%, 22%, or 33% to the ceiling; higher ranks replace lower ranks. A Fighter with these reaches 44%, 55%, or 66%. Other independent gear contributions can also help.</p><p>A Fighter protects half of a positive Strength damage modifier, rounded up, from Constitution reduction. The remaining part is reduced normally. This is a flat damage bonus, not a percentage bonus.</p><p>With a shield equipped, a Fighter also adds positive STR modifier percentage points to Block: STR +5 adds five points. All Block sources share one roll and a 33% cap. No shield means no class Block bonus.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 12</div></article><article class="page" id="rogue" data-title="Rogue: keep moving"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Rogue: keep moving</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 100%"></div><p>Rogues gain 11% ordinary voluntary movement speed in every direction, or 22% total while sprinting. The class itself adds no jump-height bonus.</p><p>Rogue Dodge contributes 11 percentage points ordinarily and 22 at the fast movement tier. Dodge needs actual voluntary speed: below one quarter of walking speed it is zero. Below 90% of sprint speed, the combined cap is 33%; at or above it, the cap is 66%. Forced movement and jumping do not manufacture extra Dodge. Magic cannot be Dodged.</p><p>Rogue damage dice have better explosion thresholds: d6 continues on 5+, d12 on 7+, and other eligible dice on their maximum. Sealed rolls such as generation or healing are not damage explosions.</p><p>A direct attack from behind Backstabs: it ignores all Constitution damage reduction and adds one damage multiple. Normal ×1 becomes ×2; aimed ×2 becomes ×3; Deadeye ×3 becomes ×4. Passive and status damage do not Backstab.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 13</div></article><article class="page" id="wizard" data-title="Wizard: spend wisely"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Wizard: spend wisely</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>A Wizard receives one extra distinct Content at Level 1 and is the only class that can acquire and cast SUMMON. All classes can use their other owned Forms and Contents.</p><p>Arcane Shield automatically diverts part of incoming resolved HP damage into Magic. It begins at 10%, adds 2.5 percentage points per Level, and stops at 50%. Intended diverted HP rounds upward. Normally one Magic pays for one diverted HP; unpaid damage still reaches HP.</p><p>Magical damage can test Arcane Integrity and shatter the shield. Physical hits can trigger Feedback under its class rules. Arcane Shattered disables the shield until recovery. These are automatic defenses, not voluntary spell casts.</p><p>Magic capacity is always 100. Intelligence improves permitted regeneration, but an open map suppresses that regeneration first. Living Aegis improves shield efficiency, not pool size. Non-Wizards can instead obtain the Mana Barrier feat family; Wizards cannot draft it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 14</div></article><article class="page" id="wizard-offense" data-title="A Wizard’s bright ideas"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A Wizard’s bright ideas</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>Arcane Surge rewards a full Magic pool. An eligible attack committed at full Magic receives a bonus equal to your positive Intelligence modifier. Its commitment snapshot matters: spending the spell’s cost does not retroactively erase an earned full-pool bonus.</p><p>Feedback is a reactive counterattack. When an eligible hit deals HP damage to a Wizard whose shield is online, Feedback’s chance equals that Wizard’s current diversion fraction. A successful response has a one-second cooldown. It rolls one d4 for each actual die in the incoming damage contract, including continuations, then adds positive INT modifier.</p><p>Feedback is Magic damage, but does not add Wisdom or recursively trigger Feedback. An Arcane Shattered shield cannot produce this response. The separate Feedback Loop feat restores Magic from eligible damage-die continuations; read its entry for the per-cast cap.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 15</div></article><article class="page" id="leveling" data-title="Grow into a legend"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Grow into a legend</h2><p>Heroes begin at Combat Level 1 with 100 base HP. Later Levels use a stored class hit die: Fighter d10, Rogue d8, Wizard d4. Constitution&#x27;s per-Level HP bonus is capped at +6. Reopening the sheet or reconnecting does not reroll growth.</p><p>The current Hero Level ceiling is the lower of 20 and Dungeon Level +3. XP earned at the temporary ceiling is banked until the ceiling rises. Level 20 uses 48,000 total XP, after which Hero XP stops.</p><p>A kill awards 40% of its XP to the finisher and shares 60% by HP damage contribution. Defeating a human Soldier does not yield Hero XP. Each deployed Hero receives rescue XP of 500 + 100 × min(Dungeon Level, 20).</p><p>Choose one of three stored feat offers at Levels 1, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, and 18. Pending choices do not reroll on reopening, death, or reconnection. Level 20 adds one class capstone choice. There are no extra Rogue feat drafts at Levels 2, 5, 10, or 15.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 16</div></article><article class="page" id="feats" data-title="Choose your specialty"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Choose your specialty</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>A feat may require permanent ability scores, a previous rank, a weapon or Magic capability, or a particular actor type. The draft filters these requirements before presenting three distinct offers. Choose one; the other two are not yours.</p><p>Ranked families usually replace their own lower rank. Separate sources add only where their rules say they do. Six repeatable fallback feats can raise individual permanent abilities when offered, up to the intrinsic score ceiling.</p><p>The reference chapters at the back include every current ordinary feat, all six fallbacks, and the nine class capstones. Their requirements and effect descriptions come from the same catalog used by the game.</p><p>Wall Jump grants four successful kicks before landing, each requiring a fresh Jump press near a static vertical wall. Holding Jump and touching a wall do not refill them. Cloud Step is a separate ability; Wall Jump takes priority when a legal wall kick is available.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 17</div></article><article class="page" id="combat" data-title="Every hit tells a story"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Every hit tells a story</h2><p>A damage expression such as 2d6 means two six-sided dice. Eligible damage dice can explode: a qualifying result adds another die, which may continue the chain. Generation, progression, healing, and other sealed rolls do not inherit damage explosions.</p><p>Strength adds once to a physical damage contract per target; Wisdom does the same for Wisdom-scaled Magic. Constitution reduces positive die contributions, with the ordinary minimum-one rule. Strength and Wisdom are flat additions, not percentage multipliers.</p><p>Aimed and Backstab multiples, equipment, elements, Dodge, Block, and Arcane Shield then matter according to their rules. A zero or immune attack does not become damaging merely because a bonus exists.</p><p>Watch the lower-right readout, or press L for the Die Log. Both display the same authoritative events: rolls, saves, damage, Dodge, status effects, regeneration, Magic, Push, XP, and more. A DODGE can be recorded even when damage is zero. Names and labels carry meaning as well as color.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 18</div></article><article class="page" id="weapons" data-title="Know your weapons"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Know your weapons</h2><table><tr><td>Crowbar</td><td>Base 1d3; no ammunition. Bash and Walloper improve it.</td></tr><tr><td>Pistol</td><td>1d4; a straightforward sidearm.</td></tr><tr><td>Shotgun</td><td>1d6 with a minimum contribution of 3 per damage die; shared shell damage and strong hit stun.</td></tr><tr><td>SMG</td><td>1d8; rapid fire builds heat.</td></tr><tr><td>Pulse Rifle / AR2</td><td>1d10 projectiles; one cartridge powers a three-shot burst.</td></tr><tr><td>Revolver / .357</td><td>1d12 with special exploding chains and penetrating shots.</td></tr></table><p>Dice are the starting point, not guaranteed final damage. Your abilities, class, feats, aimed hits, range, armor, and elemental matchup can change the outcome.</p><p>Right mouse casts your chosen spell while a normal weapon is held. It does not use the stock game&#x27;s grenade launcher or alternate-fire attack. Ordinary explosive grenades are not part of this equipment economy.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 19</div></article><article class="page" id="weapon-tricks" data-title="Fire with a little finesse"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Fire with a little finesse</h2><p>SMG heat rises as you fire. Its baseline heat threshold is six; heat cools in quarter-second steps, and overheating locks it for two seconds. Watch and listen for warm, near-overheat, and ready feedback. Heat-control feats change the weapon&#x27;s limits.</p><p>The Pulse Rifle has a 0.45-second tell before a three-projectile burst, with 0.09-second spacing and 0.25-second recovery. The whole baseline burst spends one cartridge. Burst-size feats add projectiles through this same attack.</p><p>The Revolver uses a special d12 continuation ladder: the fresh die explodes at 8+, then later thresholds descend through 7, 6, and 5 in its ordinary chain. Rogue and relevant feats change eligible thresholds. Its magazine progress builds bonuses, and its later shots can fire extra projectiles. It also has a baseline 25% chance to conserve a cartridge.</p><p>The Shotgun rolls 8 plus an independent exploding d6 for pellet count, capped at 36. Shell damage is divided into six shares and applied according to each target’s pellet hits, with the normal minimum-one rule. Its hit stun is four times the ordinary duration. The pellet-count roll is utility, not damage, so damage feats do not inflate it.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 20</div></article><article class="page" id="aim-and-cylinder" data-title="Hold steady. Then fire."><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Hold steady. Then fire.</h2><p>The Revolver can enter an aimed state after you keep position and aim still for a base 0.5 seconds. An aimed shot deals ×2. Moving, changing your aim, switching weapons, or attacking breaks or consumes the state. Faster-aim effects reduce the required hold.</p><p>Deadeye makes the Revolver’s aimed multiple ×3 and lets the other supported ordinary weapons aim for ×2. A Rogue’s qualifying Backstab then adds one more multiple. Holding right mouse is not the aiming command; right mouse is your Magic control.</p><p>The Revolver’s cylinder adds a flat bonus equal to chambers already spent: ordinarily +0 through +5. Its fifth cartridge fires two projectiles; its sixth fires three. Below 60 HP, each HP below 60 adds one percentage point of chance for one extra projectile. Below 34% maximum HP, each full percentage point below 34 adds one point of chance to preserve the final cartridge. These conditional chances are separate from its baseline 25% ammunition conservation.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 21</div></article><article class="page" id="ammo" data-title="Make every round count"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make every round count</h2><table><tr><td>Weapon</td><td>Magazine / total carried / base recovery</td></tr><tr><td>Pistol</td><td>18 / 54 / 60 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Shotgun</td><td>7 / 21 / 90 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>SMG</td><td>25 / 75 / 120 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Pulse Rifle</td><td>20 / 60 / 150 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Revolver</td><td>6 / 18 / 180 seconds</td></tr></table><p>Total carried includes the loaded magazine. All copies of one weapon family share ammunition; collecting another copy does not create an independent ammo reserve.</p><p>Passive recovery prevents complete deadlock. Below the normal one-magazine floor, ammunition returns gradually after an interruption delay. The times above are the base empty-to-floor recovery schedules; firing and applicable feats affect recovery. It is not a full-ammo refill.</p><p>Ammo pickups favor an owned family with room. Reload deliberately, use the crowbar when appropriate, and save powerful shots for threats that deserve them.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 22</div></article><article class="page" id="magic" data-title="Put a spell together"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Put a spell together</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 100%"></div><p>Open I for the Spellbook. Choose an owned Form to decide how the spell travels, then an owned Content to decide its element and rider. Cast with right mouse. The Form&#x27;s base cost and the Content&#x27;s surcharge combine before applicable cost rules.</p><p>Every Hero begins with one eligible random Form and RAW Content. Wizards receive an additional distinct Content at Level 1. The normal Content grants occur at Levels 4, 8, and 14. Only a Wizard can acquire or cast SUMMON.</p><p>Magic tops out at 100. Intelligence governs regeneration; Wisdom governs spell power and approved range, radius, and utility scaling. A spell&#x27;s visible area boundary marks its actual area, while a Beam ends where world geometry stops it.</p><p>If you are holding a Healing Potion or Stink Bomb, its throwable controls take priority. Switch back to a normal weapon to cast.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 23</div></article><article class="page" id="forms" data-title="Six Forms of Magic"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Six Forms of Magic</h2><table><tr><td>BLAST · 45</td><td>2d6; connected cells around caster. No innate Push.</td></tr><tr><td>BEAM · 20</td><td>2d6; narrow straight laser pierces bodies, stops at world geometry.</td></tr><tr><td>BOMB · 20</td><td>3d6; lobbed area attack. Throw range fixed; radius scales with WIS.</td></tr><tr><td>MISSILE · 25</td><td>3d6; steerable projectile with area impact. WIS affects range and radius.</td></tr><tr><td>BOLT · 15</td><td>4d6; fast precision shot. No area or body piercing.</td></tr><tr><td>SUMMON · 12</td><td>Wizard only: call a gold Seeker for 20 seconds. Default cap: one.</td></tr></table><p>Numbers beside names are base Magic costs before Content. A summoned Seeker carries the selected Content into its attacks. An invalid placement that cannot find safe ground and hull clearance fails without spending Magic.</p><p>Beam can hit each eligible body along its resolved line once. Its blue/cyan core remains recognizable even with an elemental accent.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 24</div></article><article class="page" id="contents" data-title="Give your spell a character"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Give your spell a character</h2><table><tr><td>RAW · +0</td><td>Untyped Magic; no Content rider</td></tr><tr><td>EARTH · +10</td><td>336-unit Push attempt</td></tr><tr><td>FIRE · +15</td><td>Immolated attempt</td></tr><tr><td>DARK · +15</td><td>Poisoned attempt</td></tr><tr><td>ICE · +10</td><td>Held attempt; Wisdom save</td></tr><tr><td>LIGHT · +5</td><td>Muted attempt</td></tr><tr><td>ELECTRIC · +10</td><td>Morale / Intimidated attempt</td></tr></table><p>A rider is an attempt, not a guaranteed condition. The attack must deal qualifying damage to a surviving target; then the appropriate save or Morale rules apply. A duplicate guaranteed rider does not secretly create repeated rolls for the same event.</p><p>Contents are class-neutral. A Fighter or Rogue may attach any Content they own to a Form they can use. Summoning itself remains Wizard-only.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 25</div></article><article class="page" id="elements" data-title="Find their weak side"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Find their weak side</h2><table><tr><td>Earth ↔ Electric</td><td>Each opposes the other</td></tr><tr><td>Fire ↔ Ice</td><td>Each opposes the other</td></tr><tr><td>Dark ↔ Light</td><td>Each opposes the other</td></tr></table><p>Each generated monster independently has a one-in-three chance of elemental affinity. If it has one, the six elements are equally likely. An Earth creature resists Earth and is weak to Electric; the same reciprocal rule applies to each pair.</p><p>The other four elements and untyped damage remain neutral unless another explicit effect says otherwise. Weakness and resistance use their own single-roll ladders, from 11% through 88%; do not assume several visible sources mean several separate reduction rolls.</p><p>An elemental aura and aimed elemental words help identify affinity. Class tint and elemental affinity are different clues: a Rogue or Wizard can have any element. Gear may add elemental damage, wards, or a weakness. Read both the benefit and the drawback.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 26</div></article><article class="page" id="conditions" data-title="Trouble you can shake off"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Trouble you can shake off</h2><table><tr><td>Clumsy · DEX</td><td>Slower movement and impaired attack handling; 1+1d4 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Immolated · DEX</td><td>Burning damage; recovery checks can extinguish it.</td></tr><tr><td>Poisoned · CON</td><td>Loses HP on cell movement; periodic saves can clear it.</td></tr><tr><td>Bleeding · CON</td><td>Repeated damage ticks with recovery saves.</td></tr><tr><td>Muted · WIS</td><td>Prevents voluntary Magic casting; 1+1d4 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Held · WIS</td><td>Stops voluntary movement; explosive 1d6 duration.</td></tr><tr><td>Reckless · WIS</td><td>Disrupts restraint; AI can turn on nearby allies.</td></tr><tr><td>Arcane Shattered · INT</td><td>Arcane Shield is offline until recovery.</td></tr><tr><td>Intimidated · CHA</td><td>Brief player disruption from failed Morale.</td></tr></table><p>The listed ability is the relevant condition-save lane. Many durations are rolled, so repeated applications need not last equally long. Status riders and feat procs still respect their saves. Reapplication follows the condition&#x27;s own refresh rule, rather than creating unlimited copies.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 27</div></article><article class="page" id="defenses" data-title="Dodge, Block, Push, Morale"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Dodge, Block, Push, Morale</h2><p>Dodge combines eligible sources into one speed-gated roll against physical attacks. Block combines eligible sources into another shared physical defense, capped at 33%. Neither is a promise of invulnerability. Wizard diversion spends Magic after ordinary mitigation.</p><p>Push is a resolved attack effect, not free movement. Strength, size, equipment, and relevant feats affect its attempt and distance. A legitimate wall collision can become a wall slam; ordinary contact with scenery is not automatically bonus damage.</p><p>Hit stun can interrupt a hostile&#x27;s preparation. Watch telegraphs: canceling a charge is different from trying to stop an attack that has already been released.</p><p>Morale compares the attacker&#x27;s Charisma pressure with the defender&#x27;s save when a qualifying trauma or effect calls for it. A failed check can make an AI flee; a player receives the authored brief Intimidated response instead of losing the controls to AI. Cooldowns prevent every pellet from demanding another Morale check.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 28</div></article><article class="page" id="equipment" data-title="Dress for the rescue"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Dress for the rescue</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>Equipment has a bag and dedicated slots. You can keep multiple copies of a weapon family and select the one to equip. Touching a pickup stores the item; it does not silently replace your carefully chosen gear.</p><p>Wearable families are headwear, vest, trousers, boots, rings, gloves, and shields. A ring can use either hand. A pair of gloves occupies both hands; displacing it frees both. The shield uses the left-arm accessory slot, not a ring slot.</p><p>Your ordinary equipment storage holds 32 records plus the two consumable stacks. Only equipped wearables and the currently wielded weapon contribute their effects. A powerful weapon sitting in the bag does nothing to your current attack.</p><p>Compare an item&#x27;s name, properties, drawback, and Value before equipping it. Unequip first if you want to trash an item. Trashing destroys the selected unequipped record, including a whole consumable stack when selected.</p><p>A weapon’s element colors its surface tint, soft aura and muzzle flash. Sparse brushed, ceramic, carbon or hammered patches preserve the stock gun and its detail. Finish and glow variations follow the saved roll; rarity strengthens the presentation. Inspect the listed properties for exact effects. Wintery means Ice, including Wintery Warding and Wintery Frailty.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 29</div></article><article class="page" id="loot" data-title="Read the fine print"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Read the fine print</h2><table><tr><td>Unusual · 65%</td><td>4 benefits; value factor 1.00</td></tr><tr><td>Rare · 25%</td><td>5 benefits; value factor 1.20</td></tr><tr><td>Exalted · 9%</td><td>6 benefits; value factor 1.45</td></tr><tr><td>Legendary · 1%</td><td>7 benefits; value factor 1.75</td></tr></table><p>Current generated gear also has one drawback and one elemental damage identity. Weapons always carry a hit rider. An item&#x27;s effect depends on its actual rolls; a grand name alone is no guarantee it suits your build.</p><p>Equipment power scales with Dungeon Level up to 999, even after Hero Level stops at 20. Quality and family affect its budget; paired gloves account for their two occupied slots. Value is a comparison score, not cash in hand.</p><p>Properties can affect abilities, saves, damage, speed, Dodge, Block, regeneration, Push, map cost, breadcrumbs, summons, elemental wards, weaknesses, and hit riders. Conditional damage bonuses say when they work: injured, almost stationary, or highly charged with Magic. The equipment-property reference lists every current property.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 30</div></article><article class="page" id="supplies" data-title="Supplies in the corridors"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Supplies in the corridors</h2><p>Ordinary drops are character-owned. A pickup you can inspect is not necessarily an item another player can claim. Walk into your eligible pickup to collect it. A full bag or full consumable stack leaves an unaccepted pickup available rather than silently destroying it.</p><p>Early dungeons offer more need-aware assistance: resource shortages and long dry spells can influence useful drops. This help decreases through Dungeons 2–10; from Dungeon 11 onward the assistance layer no longer provides pity, objective guarantees, deficit steering, or resource-budget supplements. Base random loot remains.</p><p>Normal ammo pickups add up to 6 Pistol rounds, 3 Shotgun shells, 9 SMG rounds, 6 Pulse Rifle rounds, or 2 Revolver rounds, limited by room. The carried caps still include the magazine. Equipment drops, consumables, and temporary ammunition are ordinary run inventory; they are not persistent DFTs.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 31</div></article><article class="page" id="bottles" data-title="A bottle for a friend"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A bottle for a friend</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 0%"></div><p>Healing Potions and Stink Bombs each stack up to three. Equip a stack in the Throwable slot, then select it as your held weapon. Its controls appear on screen.</p><p>Healing Potion: right mouse drinks it to restore up to 25 HP. At full ordinary HP, drinking is rejected without consuming one. Left mouse throws it; the first living allied Hero hit receives the healing. There is no healing splash. A missed throw still spends the bottle.</p><p>Stink Bomb: left mouse throws a five-second poison cloud with a 96-unit radius. Enemies entering make the normal Constitution save against Poisoned. You cannot drink a Stink Bomb.</p><p>Neither bottle spends Magic. Holding one takes over right-mouse handling; switch weapons before trying to cast a spell.</p><blockquote>“The red one is medicine. Please read the label.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 32</div></article><article class="page" id="moves" data-title="Equipment with a trick"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Equipment with a trick</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 100%"></div><table><tr><td>Quickstep · boots</td><td>↑ ↑ ↑ · 10 Magic · 2-second cooldown</td></tr><tr><td>Rebuff · ring</td><td>← ↓ → · 20 Magic · 4-second cooldown</td></tr></table><p>Quickstep dashes forward up to 90 units over 0.2 seconds; walls stop it. Ordinary voluntary-speed Dodge rules apply. It is not permission to cross a locked gate.</p><p>Rebuff sends a one-cell burst of 1d6 untyped Magic, followed by a 96-unit Push attempt against surviving damaged enemies.</p><p>You must equip an item that grants the move. Enter its sequence with the arrow keys. The default alternate keys map Up to [, Down to comma, Left to semicolon, and Right to backslash. Rebind those mirror keys in Equipment if needed.</p><p>Pause more than 0.8 seconds between inputs and the sequence resets. An unavailable move, insufficient Magic, or an active cooldown cannot be bypassed by entering the combination faster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 33</div></article><article class="page" id="death" data-title="Down, but not finished"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Down, but not finished</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><p>Heroes start with three lives. On death, your weapons, magazines, reserve ammunition, equipped wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions stay with your Hero for the respawn. Your character identity, XP, class, feats, and persistent DFT collection remain too. Reconnecting does not restore a lost life or reroll your character.</p><p>A normal death has a mandatory 20-second respawn lockout. Play Tetris while waiting, or spectate. After the lockout, press F to finalize your reward and respawn. You can keep playing until the normal 60-second death-Tetris maximum, when respawn happens automatically.</p><p>When no living or respawnable cooperative Hero remains, the campaign wipes. A human Soldier does not keep the Hero campaign alive. Defeating Gordon on the same tick as the final Hero&#x27;s loss is not a rescue; someone still has to reach Deborah.</p><p>Successful rescue can bring eliminated teammates back under the campaign&#x27;s comeback rules, up to the maximum of four lives. A revived Hero returns with the same saved weapons, ammunition, wearables, stored gear, and throwable potions.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 34</div></article><article class="page" id="tetris" data-title="Make room for a comeback"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Make room for a comeback</h2><table><tr><td>One line</td><td>+10 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Two lines at once</td><td>+30 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Three lines at once</td><td>+50 temporary HP</td></tr><tr><td>Four lines at once</td><td>+80 temporary HP</td></tr></table><p>Move pieces left or right, rotate with Up/Forward, soft-drop with Down/Back, and hard-drop with Jump. Clearing several lines at once earns the corresponding reward, not the sum of separate single-line clears.</p><p>Rewards become temporary HP overfill. They do not permanently increase maximum HP and cannot be refilled by ordinary regeneration. Topping out ends the board session while preserving the bonus already earned; it does not erase the mandatory death wait.</p><p>After rescue, Victory Tetris lasts 20 seconds. Russian Asset doubles qualifying HP rewards in both Tetris modes. It also raises the death-Tetris maximum to 120 seconds, but F still becomes available after the same 20-second lockout. It never extends Victory Tetris or the active dungeon clock.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 35</div></article><article class="page" id="soldiers" data-title="Friends—and human foes"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Friends—and human foes</h2><p>Up to four players take cooperative Hero roles. Up to six additional players can control enemy Soldiers, for ten active players; further arrivals spectate. Role availability and the Soldier queue determine when an incarnation can be entered.</p><p>A human Soldier receives a generated monster character, class, abilities, and automatically selected feats. The player does not draft Hero feats. A fresh incarnation is a fresh Soldier character, not the revival of the previous build.</p><p>Human Soldiers can earn incarnation-local XP and automatic Combat Levels, capped by Dungeon Level +3. AI Soldiers do not earn XP. Monster-side Levels can reach 999. Soldier participation does not prevent a Hero wipe.</p><p>The Debbie Fund can reward Soldier-attributable Hero life losses after a successful rescue. Switching into Soldier play forfeits that player&#x27;s Hero share for that dungeon. The fund does not award a rescue pool for a wipe.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 36</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-basics" data-title="Meet the prison staff"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Meet the prison staff</h2><p>An enemy&#x27;s archetype tells you how it fights; its class, Combat Level, feats, and element make that particular creature different. Normal tier chances are 60% Typical, 30% Elite, and 10% Champion, using Dungeon Level, +1, and +2 respectively. A tier is a Level change, not a secret second set of damage multipliers.</p><p>Shamblers threaten close quarters; Runners close gaps quickly. Deadcrabs are low jumping attackers. Soldiers use ranged fire; Blitzers press aggressively; Snipers punish exposed sight lines.</p><p>BioBlasters threaten with biological attacks. Watchers demand attention to their readable attack tells. Seekers telegraph a committed charge: move out of its path instead of retreating along it.</p><p>Listen, look ahead, and use corners. Killing an enemy does not guarantee a permanently safe floor; wandering populations can replenish. Size, class tint, elemental aura, and aimed information help you decide what to tackle first.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 37</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-new" data-title="More unwelcome company"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>More unwelcome company</h2><table><tr><td>Climber</td><td>Pink fast-zombie silhouette; climbs to reach you. Watch vertical approaches.</td></tr><tr><td>Nodule</td><td>Stationary gas hazard. Do not stand beside it.</td></tr><tr><td>Flamer</td><td>Red soldier silhouette; warns before a short-range fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Big Crab</td><td>Oversized headcrab with a telegraphed fire attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Sentry</td><td>Stationary gun emplacement; break its firing line.</td></tr><tr><td>Razor</td><td>Flying blade with a warned dive. Move off its committed approach.</td></tr><tr><td>Arc Caster</td><td>Marks a target area before its Magic strike. Leave the mark.</td></tr><tr><td>Lurker</td><td>Ceiling ambusher with a venom shot. Look up.</td></tr><tr><td>Beam Sweeper</td><td>Stationary sweeping laser. Interrupt its charge or use cover; gunfire will not cancel an already released beam.</td></tr></table><p>Fire can attempt Immolated; venom can attempt Poisoned. These enemies use the same saves, damage, elements, and progression rules as the rest of the roster.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 38</div></article><article class="page" id="neil" data-title="Neil has the Black Keycard"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Neil has the Black Keycard</h2><div class="boss-art" role="img" aria-label="Neil, Brute, and Gordon the Warden"></div><p>After Yellow Gate, look for Neil: the green-suited bureaucrat clutching a briefcase. Neil flees through the open labyrinth and across connected floors. He does not attack you. He alone carries the Black Keycard.</p><p>Brute is his independently killable antlion-guard escort. It keeps near Neil and reacts when you hit him. Orange warning marks a charge: after a 1.25-second preparation, Brute commits to its direction. Sidestep that line. A wall collision stuns it for two seconds.</p><p>You may defeat the escort first, but Brute is not an invulnerability switch. Neil can be defeated independently. Take the Black Keycard dropped by Neil to progress.</p><p>Neil is fixed at Dungeon Level. Brute is fixed at Dungeon Level +1.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 39</div></article><article class="page" id="gordon" data-title="Gordon the Warden"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Gordon the Warden</h2><div class="boss-art" role="img" aria-label="Neil, Brute, and Gordon the Warden"></div><p>Beyond Black Gate is a two-level arena: a lower court and an upper gallery. Gordon is the heavy man in a pig mask. Entering commits the encounter and closes the back gate.</p><p>An entry or respawn alcove provides protection and one aid allowance per Hero identity per dungeon: up to 25 HP, up to 30 ammunition per owned gun, and one Healing Potion. Repeated entry is not an unlimited supply.</p><p>Above roughly 60% HP, Gordon alternates a three-second cloak with a visible warning before launching four slow homing orbs. Around 60%, he rides his toilet and leaves bombs with three-second fuses. Around 25%, he clears that ordnance and charges with his crowbar.</p><p>The fight does not reset just because a Hero dies. Defeat him, take the Jail Key at the arena center, open the jail, and touch Deborah. Gordon&#x27;s death alone never completes the dungeon.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 40</div></article><article class="page" id="fund" data-title="The Debbie Fund"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The Debbie Fund</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>The Debbie Fund takes rescue funding very seriously. Its $DEB units are Debbie Coins—&#x27;Debs&#x27;—and its collectibles are Debbie Fund Tokens, or DFTs. These are server-local game records, with no cash value or outside wallet.</p><p>A successful rescue creates a pool of 100 × Dungeon Level Debs. Qualifying Soldier-attributable Hero life losses assign 11% each to the Soldier side, capped at 33% of the pool; Soldier damage contributions determine its allocation.</p><p>The remaining Hero pool is divided half equally and half by damage and effective allied healing, with hostile-loss adjustments. Eligible dead or disconnected participants can retain their claim. Switching to Soldier play forfeits a Hero share.</p><p>Rescue awards increase wallet balance and lifetime rescue earnings. DFT sales increase spendable balance but do not inflate that lifetime rescue score. The staging Heroes of Legend board records party rescue achievements; it is not a count of monster kills.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 41</div></article><article class="page" id="tokens" data-title="A little something to keep"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little something to keep</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 0%"></div><p>DFTs mint only when a rescue succeeds. Combat Level milestones 1, 5, 10, and 20 can each award one token per account on this server. A rare rescue roll also gives each participating Hero a one-in-1,000 chance. Ordinary enemy kills do not mint DFTs.</p><p>Your collection has eight slots. Milestone items waiting for space stay frozen; making room does not reroll them. Tokens persist across ordinary death and campaign restart.</p><p>At the staging Debbie statue, recreate a token&#x27;s frozen item for free once per token per run. The DFT remains in your collection. The same token becomes available to recreate again in the next run.</p><p>You may instead sell a DFT permanently for its displayed Value in Debs. Read the confirmation carefully: selling the token removes it. Wallet viewing is portable; the statue&#x27;s staging, proximity, and role rules still govern its physical services.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 42</div></article><article class="page" id="tips" data-title="Deborah&#x27;s parting advice"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Deborah&#x27;s parting advice</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><ol><li>Check the clock before lingering in staging. Every rescue belongs to the same countdown.</li><li>Keep a useful spell selected. A bottle in your hand means you are holding a bottle—not casting.</li><li>Move sideways out of committed charges. A longer corridor is not always a safer escape.</li><li>Compare gear drawbacks as carefully as benefits. Keep your permanent feat requirements in mind.</li><li>Use short map checks, read the current objective, and remember the stairs.</li><li>When the Warden falls, finish the job. Jail Key, jail door, Deborah.</li></ol><blockquote>“You&#x27;ve read the book. Now make yourself useful.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 43</div></article><article class="page" id="feat-cha_academic_achievement" data-title="Academic Achievement"><div class="kicker">Feat reference</div><h2>Academic Achievement</h2><section class="entry" id="CHA_ACADEMIC_ACHIEVEMENT"><h3>Academic Achievement</h3><p class="requirement">CHA 15 / magic_pool</p><p>Adds max(0, CHA_MOD) to INT_MOD only for the canonical passive Magic-regeneration calculation. This does not enable suspended regeneration, raise Max Magic, alter active costs, map drain, spell damage, saves, or any other INT/CHA rule.</p><p class="actors">Heroes, human Soldiers, and AI</p></section><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 44</div></article><article class="page" id="feat-cha_aggressive_personality" data-title="Aggressive Personality"><div class="kicker">Feat reference</div><h2>Aggressive Personality</h2><section class="entry" id="CHA_AGGRESSIVE_PERSONALITY"><h3>Aggressive Personality</h3><p class="requirement">CHA 15 / eligible physical attack</p><p>While ready, the next eligible physical attack adds max(0, CHA_MOD) once per resolved target and begins a sealed non-exploding 1d3-second cooldown.</p><p class="actors">Heroes, human Soldiers, and AI with an eligible attack</p></section><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 45</div></article><article class="page" id="feat-cha_aura_burst" data-title="Aura Burst"><div class="kicker">Feat reference</div><h2>Aura Burst</h2><section class="entry" id="CHA_AURA_BURST_1"><h3>Aura Burst</h3><p class="requirement">CHA 13</p><p>On one successful discrete Magic spend, deals max(0, CHA_MOD) supplemental magical damage to hostiles within 0 same-floor cell radius.</p><p class="actors">Heroes, human Soldiers, and AI with a discrete active Magic Form</p></section><section class="entry" id="CHA_RADIANCE_2"><h3>Radiance</h3><p class="requirement">CHA 15 / requires Aura Burst</p><p>On one successful discrete Magic spend, deals max(0, CHA_MOD) supplemental magical damage to hostiles within 1 same-floor cell radius.</p><p class="actors">Heroes, human Soldiers, and AI with a discrete active Magic Form</p></section><section class="entry]====]
